328 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 48 0 R
/T (C type discussion: the C standard guarantees minimum ranges, not that int is always 32 bits or long is always 64 bits. On many 64-bit Windows compilers, long is still 32 bits; on common 64-bit Linux systems, long is 64 bits. long long is at least 64 bits. Use limits from limits.h and check multiplication/addition bounds if a production system accepts unbounded input.)
/E (C type discussion: the C standard guarantees minimum ranges, not that int is always 32 bits or long is always 64 bits. On many 64-bit Windows compilers, long is still 32 bits; on common 64-bit Linux systems, long is 64 bits. long long is at least 64 bits. Use limits from limits.h and check multiplication/addition bounds if a production system accepts unbounded input.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
329 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 48 0 R
/T (Float-versus-integer comparison: a float or double can represent many decimal values only approximately. Summing a large number of bill amounts can accumulate small rounding differences. Integer paise is exact for two-decimal currency, provided overflow is prevented. A double accumulator is easy to use but requires rounding rules and is not preferred for financial totals.)
/E (Float-versus-integer comparison: a float or double can represent many decimal values only approximately. Summing a large number of bill amounts can accumulate small rounding differences. Integer paise is exact for two-decimal currency, provided overflow is prevented. A double accumulator is easy to use but requires rounding rules and is not preferred for financial totals.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [14]
>>
